# The chip answers to the configuration it was formatted against, which is not
# always the one this model ships - a handset whose secure element was
# provisioned elsewhere needs that set instead. init.sony.rc binds by model;
# this binds whatever FelicaParts was told to use on top of it.
#
# Lives on system_ext because the property has to be writable by the settings
# app: /vendor and /odm scripts expand ${} through vendor_init, which cannot
# read a system property, and a vendor property cannot be set from an app at
# all. init parses this one itself, so it can read either.
#
# The name is shared with every other device that ships this, which is why it
# carries no vendor segment: the same setprop works on joan_jp, where a model
# is spelled as an LG SKU instead.
#
# Binding over a path that is already a bind mount propagates the new mount to
# the peers of the one underneath, and the peer path is that mount's root - the
# set init.sony.rc bound at early-init. So picking another model here also
# leaves a copy of it on top of the model's own directory:
#
#   /vendor/etc/felica_SO-52B  -> /vendor/etc/felica            (init.sony.rc)
#   /vendor/etc/felica_SOG04   -> /vendor/etc/felica            (this)
#   /vendor/etc/felica_SOG04   -> /vendor/etc/felica_SO-52B     (propagated)
#
# Marking either mount private would stop it, and would also stop it reaching
# the namespace the apps live in, which is the whole point of the bind. It only
# matters when the choice is changed twice without rebooting in between, which
# is what the reboot the settings screen asks for avoids: at boot the source
# directories are untouched, so whichever set is picked binds the real one.
on property:persist.felica.model=*
    mount none /vendor/etc/felica_${persist.felica.model} /vendor/etc/felica bind
